310 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H5
/P 198 0 R
/T (In the text on which the piece is based, Alessandra Molina uses the image of the bird as an object of artistic appreciation, giving it a grotesque and visceral twist. The description of the bird highlights its animalistic, contradictory, and wild qualities, as if it were an entity completely alien to the human gaze. The idea of the fall of this bird, as described by Alessandra Molina, lies at the core of the musical material of the piece: both the voice and the piano ascend in one way and descend in another, creating a contrast between harmonic leaps and a chaotic descent full of chromaticism and erratic movements. The spoken voice is used in the piece as a parenthesis to the singing, as if it were a different points of view to the falling of this bird, revealing new qualities of the animal described.)
/E (In the text on which the piece is based, Alessandra Molina uses the image of the bird as an object of artistic appreciation, giving it a grotesque and visceral twist. The description of the bird highlights its animalistic, contradictory, and wild qualities, as if it were an entity completely alien to the human gaze. The idea of the fall of this bird, as described by Alessandra Molina, lies at the core of the musical material of the piece: both the voice and the piano ascend in one way and descend in another, creating a contrast between harmonic leaps and a chaotic descent full of chromaticism and erratic movements. The spoken voice is used in the piece as a parenthesis to the singing, as if it were a different points of view to the falling of this bird, revealing new qualities of the animal described.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
